<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the changeover-menudump helper.

  Library validation, which the hardened runtime turns on, lets a process load
  only libraries signed by the same Team ID or by Apple. The helper's whole
  decryption path is a runtime dlopen of the user's own Homebrew libdvdread,
  which is ad-hoc signed by Homebrew and therefore neither — so the first
  signed build reported `"libdvdread": { "status": "missing" }` on a Mac where
  the library was plainly installed and the unsigned build had just used it.
  A missing library and a blocked one look identical from inside the process,
  which is exactly the confusion this comment exists to end.

  The entitlement is on the helper alone, not on the app. The helper is a
  700-line program launched as a subprocess that reads a disc and exits; the
  app keeps library validation intact.

  dlopen is also why this cannot be solved by bundling: the point is to use
  whichever libdvdread the user installed, and never to ship one.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
